# Line endings.
#
# Everything textual is LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on
# every platform including a Windows checkout. Without this, git's
# core.autocrlf on Windows would check out CRLF, and the files that matter
# most here are the ones a user is invited to open in a text editor:
# watches/_template.toml, and every watch.toml a collection is made of.
#
# SAAT itself is indifferent — tomlkit parses CRLF fine, and every file is
# read and written as UTF-8 text with universal newlines, so a watch.toml
# rewritten by a Windows editor still loads on Linux. This is about the
# repository staying one shape rather than two, so a diff never turns into
# a whole-file rewrite because someone cloned on a different OS.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch and PowerShell interpreters want CRLF; Inno Setup accepts
# either but is conventionally CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Never touched: git must not go looking for line endings in a font, an
# image or a compiled translation.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.qm   binary
*.pdf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.deb  binary
*.zip  binary
